# Laravel Project Rules for Cursor IDE

## 1. CORS HEADERS - SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH
- NEVER add CORS headers in multiple places (index.php, middleware, controllers)
- Use ONLY the AddCorsHeaders middleware for CORS
- Always check if headers already exist before adding: `if (!$response->headers->has('Access-Control-Allow-Origin'))`
- CORS headers MUST be added to ALL responses, including error responses (500, 404, 422)
- Handle OPTIONS preflight requests immediately in middleware, return 200/204
- Test CORS in browser, not just curl (curl doesn't enforce CORS)

## 2. API ROUTE STRUCTURE - REGION-BASED
- All authenticated API routes use region prefix: `/api/v1/{local|intl}/`
- Routes are defined in a foreach loop for both 'local' and 'intl'
- Always use `ensure.region.match` middleware for region-specific routes
- Public routes (login, register) are at `/api/v1/` level (no region prefix)
- Follow existing pattern: Route::prefix($prefix)->group() with auth middleware

## 3. ERROR HANDLING - ALWAYS INCLUDE CORS
- Wrap all controller methods in try-catch blocks
- Log errors with context: user_id, request URI, error message, stack trace
- Return JSON responses with proper HTTP status codes
- ALWAYS add CORS headers to error responses via exception handlers in bootstrap/app.php
- Use Laravel's exception handler to catch all exceptions and add CORS headers
- Never return errors without CORS headers (browser will block them)

## 4. AUTHENTICATION - JWT TOKENS
- Use JWT authentication via `auth('api')` guard
- Token is returned as `access_token` in login response
- All protected routes use `auth:api` middleware
- User is accessed via `$request->user()` in controllers
- Never expose sensitive user data in responses (password, tokens)

## 5. FILE PERMISSIONS - STORAGE DIRECTORY
- Storage directory MUST be writable by www-data (PHP-FPM user)
- Always set permissions: `chown -R www-data:www-data storage bootstrap/cache`
- Set permissions: `chmod -R 775 storage bootstrap/cache`
- Log files in storage/logs/ must be writable by PHP-FPM
- Check permissions when encountering "Permission denied" errors

## 6. CODE ORGANIZATION - CONTROLLERS & SERVICES
- API controllers go in `app/Http/Controllers/Api/`
- Use Services for business logic (app/Services/)
- Controllers should be thin - delegate to services
- Use Traits for shared functionality (DetectsRegion, AddsCorsHeaders)
- Follow existing naming: ControllerNameApiController.php

## 7. DATABASE QUERIES - ELOQUENT PATTERNS
- Use Eloquent relationships, not raw queries when possible
- Eager load relationships to avoid N+1 queries: `->with('relation')`
- Use query scopes for reusable query logic
- Always filter by user_id for user-specific data
- Use `firstOrCreate` for upsert operations, `firstOrFail` for required records

## 8. RESPONSE FORMAT - CONSISTENT JSON
- All API responses return JSON: `response()->json([...])`
- Include CORS headers on all responses (handled by middleware)
- Use consistent error format: `{'error': 'message'}` or `{'message': 'text'}`
- Return proper HTTP status codes: 200 (success), 201 (created), 400 (bad request), 401 (unauthorized), 404 (not found), 422 (validation), 500 (server error)
- Include relevant data in success responses

## 9. VALIDATION & SECURITY
- Always validate input using `$request->validate([...])`
- Use Laravel's built-in validation rules
- Sanitize user input before database operations
- Never trust client input - validate on server side
- Use rate limiting middleware for sensitive endpoints: `->middleware('throttle:60,1')`
- Check user permissions before allowing actions

## 10. LOGGING & DEBUGGING
- Use Laravel's Log facade: `Log::info()`, `Log::error()`, `Log::warning()`
- Include context in logs: user_id, request data, relevant IDs
- Log important operations: login attempts, API calls, errors
- Use structured logging with arrays for better searchability
- Never log sensitive data (passwords, tokens, credit cards)
- Check storage/logs/laravel.log for debugging (ensure permissions are correct)

## ADDITIONAL GUIDELINES
- Keep solutions simple - avoid overengineering
- Follow existing code patterns in the project
- Test endpoints with curl before assuming they work
- Always test in browser for CORS issues (curl doesn't enforce CORS)
- When fixing errors, ensure CORS headers are still present
- Use consistent file/folder naming conventions
- Document complex logic with comments
- Prefer Laravel's built-in features over custom solutions when possible

